In this lesson, we'll look at how time zones work and what happens when you cross the international date line. The world is split into zones where everyone agrees on the same clock time, and neighbouring zones usually differ by one hour. Each zone has an offset that tells you how many hours it is ahead of or behind UTC. For example, Eastern Australia is UTC plus ten in winter, and plus eleven during daylight saving. London is UTC plus zero in winter, while New York is UTC minus five. To convert from one zone to another, you work out the hour difference, then add or subtract. If your answer reaches twenty-four or more, you subtract twenty-four hours and add a day. If it goes negative, you add twenty-four hours and go back a day. Speaking of days, the international date line runs through the Pacific. Cross it heading west, from Australia towards the Americas, and you add a day because you're catching up. Cross it heading east and you subtract a day. Now let's see it in action.